## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a minimal plugin-style system that can be extended at runtime without touching core logic, a common pattern in language servers, devtools, and logging suites. It probes interfaces, registries, and non-blocking dispatch.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin registry and factories
- Interface-based extensibility
- Async dispatch to avoid blocking
- Log level mapping and dynamic enable/disable

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch in TypeScript
interface LoggerDestination {
  log(level: number, message: string): void;
}
class DestinationRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, () => LoggerDestination>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => LoggerDestination) {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }
  create(name: string): LoggerDestination {
    const f = this.factories.get(name);
    if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown destination: ${name}`);
    return f();
  }
}
class ConsoleDestination implements LoggerDestination {
  log(level: number, message: string) {
    console.log(`[${level}] ${message}`);
  }
}
class RemoteDestination implements LoggerDestination {
  constructor(private url: string) {}
  log(level: number, message: string) {
    // non-blocking demo emit
    navigator.sendBeacon?.(this.url, JSON.stringify({ level, message }));
  }
}
class MultiLogger {
  constructor(private destinations: LoggerDestination[]) {}
  log(level: number, message: string) {
    this.destinations.forEach(d => Promise.resolve().then(() => d.log(level, message)));
  }
  info(msg: string) { this.log(2, msg); }
  error(msg: string) { this.log(4, msg); }
}
```

```typescript
// Usage
const registry = new DestinationRegistry();
registry.register('console', () => new ConsoleDestination());
registry.register('remote', () => new RemoteDestination('/log'));
const logger = new MultiLogger([registry.create('console'), registry.create('remote')]);
logger.info('Startup complete');
```

